    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d568">

                <pb facs="0617" n="567" sameAs="frus:frus1894/pg567"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Baker to Mr. Gresham.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Managua</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1894-06-21"><hi rendition="#italic">June 21,
                        1894</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 315.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I think it proper to submit the following
                    correspondence, which passed between this legation and the United States
                    consulate at San Salvador, chiefly bearing on the demand of that Government for
                    the surrender of Gen. Antonio Ezeta and others who recently sought an asylum on
                    board the U. S. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Bennington.</hi></p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#italic">Cable from Consul Pollock to
                            Minister Baker.</hi></p>
                    <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">La Libertad</hi>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">June 16, 1894</hi>, (1:22 p.m.)<lb/>(Received at
                        Managua 6 p.m., June 17, 1894.)</p>
                    <p rendition="#flush">Minister <hi rendition="#small-caps">Baker</hi>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Managua:</hi></p>
                    <p>Provisional Government of Salvador just notifies me it is organized. Antonio
                        Ezeta and several officers are refugees on <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Bennington</hi>, and Salvador asked Washington for their surrender.
                        Ezeta is charged with crimes that would shock humanity.</p>
                    <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Pollock.</hi></p>
                </quote>
                <p>To which I responded: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Managua</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">June 17, 1894.</hi></p>
                        <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Pollock</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">U. S. Consul</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >San Salvador:</hi></p>
                        <p>Do not forget that you have no diplomatic powers.</p>
                        <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lewis Baker</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">U. S. Minister.</hi></p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>On the 20th Mr. Pollock responded: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">La Libertad</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">June 20, 1894</hi> (1:15 p.m.).</p>
                        <p rendition="#flush">Minister <hi rendition="#small-caps">Baker</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Managua:</hi></p>
                        <p>Have exercised no diplomatic duties whatever.</p>
                        <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Pollock.</hi></p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>On the 21st of June I addressed Mr. Pollock the inclosed communication (No. 1) in
                    explanation of my rather suggestive cable of the 17th instant.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lewis Baker.</hi></signed></closer>



